# {{project.display_name}} — CI/CD setup checklist

This project ships with a GitHub Actions workflow that deploys to three tiers
on branch events:

| Branch | Tier | GCP project |
|---|---|---|
| `{{cicd.branch_dev}}` | dev | `{{cicd.dev_gcp}}` |
| `{{cicd.branch_staging}}` | staging | `{{cicd.prod_gcp}}` |
| `{{cicd.branch_prod}}` | prod | `{{cicd.prod_gcp}}` |

The actual deploy logic lives in [`HorizonMedia/agentq-actions`](https://github.com/HorizonMedia/agentq-actions). This project's `.github/workflows/agentq-deploy.yml`
is a thin caller of that reusable workflow.

## One-time bootstrap (ops checklist)

Run **once per GCP project** by someone with project-level IAM authority.

### 1. Bootstrap the dev GCP

```bash
agentq setup-cicd \
  --gcp-project {{cicd.dev_gcp}} \
  --github-org <YOUR_ORG> \
  --github-repo <THIS_REPO_NAME> \
  --tiers dev
```

This creates:
- WIF pool `agentq-pool` + provider `github`
- Service accounts: `agentq-deploy-dev`, `agentq-runtime-dev`, `agentq-plan`
- State bucket `{{cicd.dev_state_bucket}}`
- IAM bindings (deploy SA can impersonate runtime SA, etc.)

The command prints the `workload_identity_provider` URI **for the dev GCP** and
the exact placeholder to replace (`REPLACE_ME_dev_gcp_wif_provider`). **Copy it.**

### 2. Bootstrap the prod GCP
{{#flags.gitops_enabled}}

{{#cicd.prod_gcp}}
```bash
agentq setup-cicd \
  --gcp-project {{cicd.prod_gcp}} \
  --github-org <YOUR_ORG> \
  --github-repo <THIS_REPO_NAME> \
  --tiers staging --tiers prod
```
{{/cicd.prod_gcp}}

Creates the same shape on the prod GCP, with one deploy SA per tier
(staging + prod) so each has its own IAM scope. It prints a **second**
`workload_identity_provider` URI — the one for the prod GCP — plus its
placeholder (`REPLACE_ME_prod_gcp_wif_provider`).

### 3. Paste **both** WIF URIs into `.github/workflows/agentq-deploy.yml`

The dev and prod GCPs each have their own `agentq-pool`, and SA impersonation
is authorized per-project. The workflow therefore routes `workload_identity_provider`
by environment (just like `deployer_service_account`), so you must fill **both**
slots:

- Replace `REPLACE_ME_dev_gcp_wif_provider` with the URI from step 1 (dev GCP).
- Replace `REPLACE_ME_prod_gcp_wif_provider` with the URI from step 2 (prod GCP).

> If dev and prod share a single GCP, both slots use the same placeholder token
> — replace it once with that GCP's URI.

### 3b. Provision application secrets (once per GCP)

Runtime secrets (e.g. API keys, OAuth client secrets) are **not** deployed as
plaintext `env_vars` — those land in `state.yaml`. Instead each secret lives in
Secret Manager and is referenced by a `<KEY>_SECRET_REF` env var. Because
`runtime.env_vars` is global (not per-tier), use a **project-relative** ref so
each tier reads its OWN project's copy:

```yaml
# in agentq.config.yaml → runtime.env_vars
MY_API_KEY_SECRET_REF: projects/{project}/secrets/my-api-key/versions/latest
```

`{project}` is substituted at runtime with the deployed tier's project. That
means the secret must exist — with the runtime SA granted access — in **every**
GCP the agent deploys to. Provision the shell + IAM with `setup-cicd --secret`
(re-run per GCP; it's idempotent), then add the value:

```bash
# dev GCP
agentq setup-cicd --gcp-project {{cicd.dev_gcp}} --github-org <ORG> \
  --github-repo <THIS_REPO_NAME> --tiers dev --secret my-api-key
# prod GCP
agentq setup-cicd --gcp-project {{cicd.prod_gcp}} --github-org <ORG> \
  --github-repo <THIS_REPO_NAME> --tiers staging --tiers prod --secret my-api-key

# add the VALUE in each GCP (never commit it):
printf %s "$MY_API_KEY" | gcloud secrets versions add my-api-key --project=<gcp> --data-file=-
```

Verify each tier can actually read its secrets **before** deploying:

```bash
agentq doctor --tier dev
agentq doctor --tier staging
agentq doctor --tier prod
```

`doctor --tier <t>` fails if a referenced secret is missing in that tier's
project or the runtime SA lacks `roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor` — catching
the "works in dev, denied in staging/prod" failure before it reaches runtime.

> Env vars are baked into the engine at deploy time, so after changing a secret
> **ref** (not the value) you must redeploy for it to take effect. Rotating the
> secret **value** on a `versions/latest` ref needs no redeploy.

### 4. Bootstrap KB datastores (per AgentQ project, not per GCP)

Datastores are per-agent-use-case, not bootstrap. Run from your laptop with
the appropriate `--allow-prod-kb-mutation` if staging/prod:

```bash
# dev
agentq kb create-datastore --tier dev
agentq kb create-bucket    --tier dev
agentq kb upload           --tier dev
agentq kb import           --tier dev

# staging + prod (only on initial setup; subsequent KB updates flow through GitOps)
agentq kb create-datastore --tier staging --allow-prod-kb-mutation
agentq kb create-bucket    --tier staging --allow-prod-kb-mutation
agentq kb create-datastore --tier prod    --allow-prod-kb-mutation
agentq kb create-bucket    --tier prod    --allow-prod-kb-mutation
```

### 5. Configure GitHub branch protections

For each protected branch:

| Branch | Protection rules |
|---|---|
| `{{cicd.branch_prod}}` | Require PR before merge. Require status check `agentq-deploy / deploy` (on the staging tier) to be passing. Restrict who can push. |
| `{{cicd.branch_staging}}` | Require PR. Require `agentq-deploy / deploy` (on dev) passing. |
| `{{cicd.branch_dev}}` | Require PR check passing. |

### 6. Migrate an existing engine (optional)

If you already have a deployed engine you want to bring under GitOps:

```bash
agentq state import \
  --tier prod \
  --resource-name projects/.../reasoningEngines/123 \
  --with-kb
```

This stamps the existing engine into the prod tier's state file so the next
CI run sees no drift.

## Daily workflow

1. Open a PR against `{{cicd.branch_dev}}`. CI runs `agentq state plan` and posts the diff as a PR comment.
2. Merge → CI deploys to the dev tier.
3. Open a PR `{{cicd.branch_dev}}` → `{{cicd.branch_staging}}`. Plan-only on the staging tier.
4. Merge → CI deploys to staging.
5. Open a PR `{{cicd.branch_staging}}` → `{{cicd.branch_prod}}`. Plan-only on prod. **Gate**: CI verifies that the staging tier was deployed from an ancestor of the prod branch HEAD; if not, the workflow fails.
6. Merge → CI deploys to prod. KB mutations are allowed only on this merge.

## Drift detection

If the deployed engine diverges from the state file (e.g. someone manually
edits in the Cloud console), CI fails with a clear diff. To override on the
PR, add the label `force-apply`. To override on a push, re-run with
`workflow_dispatch` and pass `dry_run: false`.

## Rollback

Roll back the engine by reverting the merge that deployed the bad change. CI
will re-deploy the reverted state. State files are versioned in GCS (30-day
noncurrent retention) so you can also `gsutil ls -a gs://.../state.yaml` and
restore an older generation if needed.
{{/flags.gitops_enabled}}
